fix_white_piece_fill_assertion.s:
Fix the stale assertion in `src/routes/chess/learn/pieces.test.ts` (second `it`, lines 19-25).

CURRENT:
	it('keeps white pieces on the old medium dark shade', () => {
		for (const i of p) {
			expect(readFileSync(resolve(process.cwd(), `static/pieces/gioco/w${i}.svg`), 'utf8')).toContain(
				'fill="#d9c8b5"'
			);
		}
	});

MEASURED FACTS: `wQ/wR/wB/wN/wP.svg` each contain the presentation attribute `fill="#d9c8b5"`. `wK.svg` does NOT — it was redrawn in Inkscape 1.4.2 and expresses the same colour through a `style` attribute instead:
  style="paint-order:fill markers stroke;fill:#d9c8b5;fill-opacity:1"
  style="fill:#d9c8b5;fill-opacity:1"
alongside a leftover `fill="#4b403b"` presentation attribute. A CSS declaration in `style` beats a presentation attribute, so **wK renders the correct light shade — there is no visual bug**. Verified: `grep -c 'fill:#d9c8b5' static/pieces/gioco/wK.svg` prints `2`.

FIX: make the assertion agnostic to which form expresses the colour. Replace lines 19-25 verbatim with:
	it('keeps white pieces on the old medium dark shade', () => {
		for (const i of p) {
			expect(readFileSync(resolve(process.cwd(), `static/pieces/gioco/w${i}.svg`), 'utf8')).toMatch(
				/fill(="|:)#d9c8b5/
			);
		}
	});

WHY NOT edit `wK.svg`: the file renders correctly; hand-editing an Inkscape export to satisfy a naive substring match risks breaking paths for zero visual gain.

DO NOT TOUCH the first `it` in this file — it already passes (`grep -c 'url(#' static/pieces/gioco/wK.svg` prints `0`, and there is no `<linearGradient`).

PHASES:
1. `pnpm test src/routes/chess/learn/pieces.test.ts` — watch the second test fail on `wK` only.
2. Apply the edit.
3. Re-run — both tests pass.
4. `pnpm test` — failures drop from 22 to 21.

COMMIT: `test: accept the style-attribute form of the white piece fill`

restore_icon_button_aria_labels.s:
This is a REAL accessibility regression found while diagnosing `learn.e2e.test.ts` — fix the CODE, not the test.

ROOT CAUSE: when the learn UI was rebuilt into `src/components/learn/*`, its icon-only buttons were given `title="…"` but lost their `aria-label`. Each button's only child is an `<svg>` icon component (`$lib/components/icons/*-icon.svelte`), so it has no text content; `title` is not a reliable accessible name for such a button and most screen readers announce nothing. The old implementation had `aria-label`, which is why `learn.e2e.test.ts` still asserts strings like `aria-label="Undo move"`.

FIX: add an `aria-label` with the SAME text, immediately after the existing `title` attribute, on every one of these. KEEP `title` (it supplies the hover tooltip). Change nothing else — no classes, no handlers.

- `src/components/learn/GameActions.svelte`: line 22 `title="New game"`, 25 `title="Undo move"`, 28 `title="Redo move"`, 32 `title="Hide hints"`, 36 `title="Show hint"`, 41 `title="Token usage"`, 45 `title="Clear chat"`, 49 `title="Settings"`
- `src/components/learn/ChatPanel.svelte`: line 70 `title="Send this message now"`, 73 `title="Remove queued message"`, 80 `title="Append selected text to message"`, 106 `title="Send"`
- `src/components/learn/BoardNavigation.svelte`: line 21 `title="Previous board"`, 24 `title="Next board"`, 27 `title="Flip board"`, 30 `title="Switch sides"`
- `src/components/learn/HintLabel.svelte`: line 15 `title="Explain hint"`

TEST FIRST — create `src/components/learn/a11y.test.ts`:
import { readFileSync, readdirSync } from 'node:fs';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';
import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';

const dir = resolve(process.cwd(), 'src/components/learn');

describe('learn icon buttons', () => {
	it('gives every titled control a matching aria-label', () => {
		for (const f of readdirSync(dir).filter((n) => n.endsWith('.svelte'))) {
			const src = readFileSync(resolve(dir, f), 'utf8');
			for (const [, label] of src.matchAll(/title="([^"]+)"/g)) {
				expect(src, `${f}: title="${label}" has no aria-label`).toContain(`aria-label="${label}"`);
			}
		}
	});
});

PHASES:
1. Create the test, run `pnpm test src/components/learn/a11y.test.ts` — watch it fail naming GameActions / ChatPanel / BoardNavigation / HintLabel.
2. Add the `aria-label` attributes.
3. Re-run — passes.
4. `pnpm test` — no previously-passing test may break. Failures stay at 21 (all in `learn.e2e.test.ts`) plus this new file now green.
5. `.log` exists at the repo root and holds live dev-server output — tail it and confirm no new Svelte compile error was introduced.

COMMIT: `a11y: give learn icon-only buttons aria-labels matching their titles`

BROWSER CHECK (was an unverified "b" gate — confirm it by hand or turn it into an agent-browser grep):
open the learn page, inspect the toolbar buttons (new game / undo / redo / hint / settings / clear chat): each icon-only button exposes an accessible name, not an empty one

rewrite_search_puzzles_for_d1.s:
Replace Qdrant with D1 in `src/lib/server/puzzles.ts` AND update both callers in the SAME step, so the repo never sits broken.

REPLACE the entire contents of `src/lib/server/puzzles.ts` (currently 71 lines of Qdrant client code) with:

import { Chess } from 'chess.js';
import type { Puzzle, PuzzleQuery } from '$lib/types/puzzle';

const MAX_ID = 916132592;
const B62 = '0123456789ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz';

type Row = { i: number; f: string; m: string; r: number; v: number; t: string };

function puzzle_id(i: number): string {
	let s = '';
	for (let k = 0; k < 5; k++) {
		s = B62[i % 62] + s;
		i = Math.floor(i / 62);
	}
	return s;
}

function build(query: PuzzleQuery, after: number, limit: number): [string, unknown[]] {
	const all = query.t ?? [];
	const p: unknown[] = [];
	let from = 'puz p';
	let id = 'p.i';
	if (all.length) {
		from = 'puz_t a0';
		id = 'a0.i';
		for (let k = 1; k < all.length; k++) {
			from += ` join puz_t a${k} on a${k}.i = a0.i and a${k}.t = ?`;
			p.push(all[k]);
		}
		from += ' join puz p on p.i = a0.i';
		p.push(all[0]);
	}
	const where: string[] = [];
	if (all.length) where.push('a0.t = ?');
	where.push(`${id} > ?`);
	p.push(after);
	if (query.r_min != null) {
		where.push('p.r >= ?');
		p.push(query.r_min);
	}
	if (query.r_max != null) {
		where.push('p.r <= ?');
		p.push(query.r_max);
	}
	where.push('p.v >= ?');
	p.push(query.v_min ?? 80);
	if (query.any?.length) {
		where.push(`p.i in (select i from puz_t where t in (${query.any.map(() => '?').join(',')}))`);
		p.push(...query.any);
	}
	p.push(limit);
	return [
		`select p.i, p.f, p.m, p.r, p.v, p.t from ${from} where ${where.join(' and ')} order by ${id} limit ?`,
		p
	];
}

export async function search_puzzles(db: D1Database, query: PuzzleQuery): Promise<Puzzle[]> {
	const limit = Math.min(Math.max(query.n ?? 5, 1), 30);
	const start = query.after ?? Math.floor(Math.random() * MAX_ID);
	const [sql, params] = build(query, start, limit);
	const rows = [...((await db.prepare(sql).bind(...params).all<Row>()).results ?? [])];
	if (rows.length < limit && query.after == null) {
		const [sql2, params2] = build(query, 0, limit);
		const seen = new Set(rows.map((r) => r.i));
		for (const r of (await db.prepare(sql2).bind(...params2).all<Row>()).results ?? []) {
			if (rows.length >= limit) break;
			if (!seen.has(r.i)) rows.push(r);
		}
	}
	return rows.map((row) => {
		const moves = row.m.split(' ');
		const p = puzzle_id(row.i);
		return {
			p,
			f: solve_fen(row.f, moves[0]),
			m: moves.slice(1).join(' '),
			r: row.r,
			v: row.v,
			u: `https://lichess.org/training/${p}`,
			t: row.t ? row.t.split(' ') : []
		};
	});
}

// A lichess puzzle's stored FEN is the position *before* the opponent's blunder. The first
// stored move is that blunder; the user solves from the position after it.
function solve_fen(fen: string, first: string): string {
	const c = new Chess(fen);
	c.move({ from: first.slice(0, 2), to: first.slice(2, 4), promotion: first.slice(4) || undefined });
	return c.fen();
}

CRITICAL TRAP: the `?` placeholders bind positionally in the order they appear in the FINAL SQL STRING, and the FROM clause is emitted before the WHERE clause. That is why `all[0]` is pushed while still building `from`, even though its `a0.t = ?` placeholder is written in `where`. Never reorder a SQL fragment without moving its `p.push` with it.

WHY a `puz_t` join table rather than FTS5: exact tag matching with no tokenizer surprises (opening tags look like `Sicilian_Defense_Najdorf_Variation`, which FTS5's default tokenizer would split on `_`), a pure index range scan on the `(t, i)` primary key, and `i > ?` random-offset sampling that the planner can push down. FTS5 would be a smaller index but its rowid-range pushdown is not guaranteed.

CALLERS:
- `src/lib/util/chat/tools/find_puzzles.ts`: keep `puzzle_input` exactly as-is and keep `after` OUT of it (it is internal pagination, not something the AI may set). Replace the exported `find_puzzles` const with a factory, because an AI SDK `tool()` is constructed at module load and has no access to `platform`:
export function make_find_puzzles(db: D1Database) {
	return tool({
		description: PUZZLE_TOOL_DESCRIPTION,
		inputSchema: puzzle_input,
		execute: async (q) => {
			try {
				const puzzles = await search_puzzles(db, q);
				if (!puzzles.length) return { puzzles: [], error: 'No puzzles matched. Try fewer tags in "t".' };
				return { puzzles };
			} catch (e) {
				return { puzzles: [], error: e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e) };
			}
		}
	});
}
- `src/routes/chess/learn/chat/+server.ts`: line 13 `import { find_puzzles } from '$lib/util/chat/tools/find_puzzles';` becomes `import { make_find_puzzles } from '$lib/util/chat/tools/find_puzzles';`; line 90 `tools: { get_fen, evaluate_position, find_puzzles },` becomes `tools: { get_fen, evaluate_position, find_puzzles: make_find_puzzles(platform!.env.PUZ) },`. `platform` is already destructured in the handler signature on line 62.
- `src/routes/api/puzzles/+server.ts`: line 6 `async ({ request })` becomes `async ({ request, platform })`; line 10 `search_puzzles(parsed.data)` becomes `search_puzzles(platform!.env.PUZ, parsed.data)`.
- `src/lib/util/chat/tools/gemini_live_dispatcher.ts` needs NO change — it POSTs `/api/puzzles` and never imports server code.

TESTS — `git rm src/lib/server/puzzles.test.ts`. That file mocks `$env/static/private` and `@qdrant/js-client-rest`, neither of which this module imports any more, so it is deleted rather than edited. Its replacement is staged and copied in below. Drive a fake D1 that records calls and returns pages the test primes, e.g.:
const calls: { sql: string; params: unknown[] }[] = [];
let pages: Row[][] = [];
const db = {
	prepare: (sql: string) => ({
		bind: (...params: unknown[]) => ({
			all: async () => {
				calls.push({ sql, params });
				return { results: pages.shift() ?? [] };
			}
		})
	})
} as unknown as D1Database;
Keep coverage of every behaviour the Qdrant version had, now asserted against SQL + bind params:
1. all-match tags emit one `join puz_t aN` per extra tag and bind them in order, with `a0.t = ?` bound to `t[0]`
2. `any` tags emit `p.i in (select i from puz_t where t in (?,?))` with one placeholder per tag
3. `r_min`/`r_max` emit `p.r >= ?` / `p.r <= ?`, and the popularity floor defaults to 80
4. the count clamps to 1..30, default 5 — assert the LAST bound param
5. successive calls use different random offsets, each `< 916132592`
6. an explicit `after` suppresses randomisation and issues exactly ONE query
7. a short first page triggers a second query whose offset is 0 and dedupes by `i`
8. row mapping: a row `{ i: 8, f: '5rk1/1p3ppp/pq3b2/8/8/1P1Q1N2/P4PPP/3R2K1 w - - 2 27', m: 'd3d6 f8d8 d6d8 f6d8', r: 1579, v: 96, t: 'advantage endgame' }` maps to `p === '00008'`, `u === 'https://lichess.org/training/00008'`, `f === '5rk1/1p3ppp/pq1Q1b2/8/8/1P3N2/P4PPP/3R2K1 b - - 3 27'`, `m === 'f8d8 d6d8 f6d8'`, `t` deep-equals `['advantage','endgame']`. NOTE `d3d6` is a QUIET queen move, not a capture — an earlier version of this test wrongly expected `pQ3b2` and the code was right.
